      "heading": "A filename is a pointer to an inode",
      "narration": "To understand links you need one idea underneath them, and it is a genuinely clarifying one. The file — the actual data, its size, its owner, its permissions, its timestamps — lives in a structure called an inode, and the inode has no name. Names live separately, in directories. A directory is really just a table mapping names to inode numbers. So a filename is not the file. It is a pointer to the file. Once you see that, several things that seemed strange become obvious. Why is renaming a file instant even if the file is fifty gigabytes? Because you only edited one row in a table; the data never moved. Why does the deletion command in the C library get called unlink? Because that is literally what it does — it removes a name, not data. The kernel keeps a count of how many names point at each inode, and only when that count drops to zero does the actual data get freed. And here is a consequence that will genuinely help you one day: if a process still has the file open, the data survives even after every name is gone. That is why deleting a huge log file sometimes does not give you your disk space back until you restart the service holding it open.",
