      "heading": "Hard link vs symbolic link",
      "narration": "Now the two kinds of link, and the difference is entirely about that inode picture. A hard link is simply a second name pointing at the same inode. The two names are peers — there is no original and no copy, and nothing in the system records which one came first. Delete either and the other keeps working perfectly, because the data only disappears when the last name goes. A symbolic link, or symlink, is a completely different animal. It is a tiny file whose entire contents are a path — a signpost that says look over there. When you open it, the kernel reads the path and follows it. Because it stores a path rather than pointing at an inode, a symlink can point across to a different disk, and it can point at a directory, neither of which a hard link can do. But it can also dangle: delete what it points at and the symlink remains, now pointing at nothing, and you get a no such file error even though the link is clearly sitting right there. In practice you will use symlinks nearly all the time and hard links almost never — but knowing the difference is what lets you diagnose a dangling link instead of staring at it.",
